DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The Kant-Herder controversy of the 1780s constitutes the founding dispute of
modern philosophy of history. The controversy erupted publicly when Kant
published two sharply critical reviews of Herder's Ideas for the Philosophy
of the History of Humanity (Ideen, 1784-91) in January 1785 and November 1785.
Kant objected that Herder replaced determinate concepts with analogies,
imported teleological speculation into science without critical warrant, and
conflated the observable organic world with a hypothetical progression of
souls or rational forces. Herder responded by embedding a counter-philosophy
of history into the Ideen itself: where Kant sought a single line of moral
progress culminating in a cosmopolitan legal order, Herder argued that each
Volk achieves its own distinctive Humanitaet -- a qualitative flowering of
human capacities -- and that no single culture or epoch can be treated as
merely instrumental for another.

The secondary literature clusters around three contested questions: (1) the
precise methodological and metaphysical content of their disagreement over
teleology, organism, and analogy in natural history; (2) the normative stakes
of Herder's pluralism versus Kant's universalism (relativism vs. Eurocentrism;
moral progress vs. cultural particularity); and (3) the reception history,
asking whether the Kantian or Herderian strand proved more philosophically
productive for subsequent German thought. Scholars like Zammito (2002) and
Nassar (2015) have reconstructed the methodological dimensions of the dispute;
Sikka (2007, 2011), Barnard (2003), and Denby (2005) address the normative
stakes; Beiser (2011), Gjesdal (2017) assess the legacy.
A persistent unresolved tension concerns whether Herder's organic pluralism
escapes relativism or collapses into it.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
The Kant-Herder debate establishes the foundational fault lines that
structure debates about Kant's philosophy of history: Herder's objections
expose the assumptions embedded in Kant's 1784 Idea essay (teleological
naturalism, universalism, the instrumentalization of individuals and cultures)
and force confrontation with the question of whether any normative philosophy
of history can avoid these commitments. Understanding what Kant is arguing for
requires understanding what Herder argues against.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
The specifically political dimensions of the Kant-Herder dispute -- concerning
state sovereignty, federalism, and cosmopolitan law -- are less well covered
than the metaphysical and teleological dimensions. The reception of the dispute
in non-German contexts (Anglophone philosophy of history, postcolonial theory)
is also underrepresented. Entries marked INCOMPLETE: zammito2002kant (no
abstract available), helbig2016epigenesis (no abstract available),
zuckert2011history (no abstract available), schmidt2025legacy (no abstract
available).

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Treat the Kant-Herder dispute as the primary frame for assessing the
presuppositions of Kant's 1784 essay. The key question for synthesis is
whether Herder's objections are decisive against Kant's approach, or whether
Kant's critical teleology (developed after 1790 in response to Herder) offers
a more defensible account. Secondary literature provides both pro-Herder
(Zammito, Barnard, Sikka) and pro-Kant (Beiser, McNulty) readings.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Pro-Herder pluralism: 4 papers (Zammito 2002, Barnard 2003, Sikka 2007/2011, Denby 2005)
- Pro-Kant or balanced critical teleology: 3 papers (Beiser 2011, McNulty 2022, Zuckert 2011)
- Methodological/analogy focus: 3 papers (Nassar 2015, Helbig/Nassar 2016, Mertel 2016)
- Historical reception: 2 papers (Gjesdal 2017, Schmidt 2025)
====================================================================
}

@book{zammito2002kant,
  author = {Zammito, John H.},
  title = {Kant, Herder, and the Birth of Anthropology},
  year = {2002},
  publisher = {University of Chicago Press},
  address = {Chicago},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Zammito reconstructs the intellectual relationship between Kant and Herder from Herder's student days in Koenigsberg through the public controversy of the 1780s, arguing that the "precritical" Kant was far more philosophically influential than is usually acknowledged, and that Herder represents a philosophically serious rival whose reformulation of Kantian empiricism gave birth to modern anthropology as a discipline.

  RELEVANCE: This is the most extensively researched study of the Kant-Herder relationship in English, providing the essential historical and philosophical context for the 1784-85 controversy. It establishes that the dispute is not merely a personal quarrel but reflects a deep methodological disagreement about the use of analogy, teleology, and empirical inquiry in understanding human nature and history.

  POSITION: Pro-Herder: argues that Herder's critique of Kant's apriorism is philosophically well-founded and that the two thinkers represent genuinely different philosophical programs, not a superior and inferior approach to similar questions.
  },
  keywords = {Kant-Herder-controversy, anthropology, teleology, philosophy-of-history, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{barnard2003herder,
  author = {Barnard, F. M.},
  title = {Herder on Nationality, Humanity, and History},
  year = {2003},
  publisher = {McGill-Queen's University Press},
  address = {Montreal},
  doi = {10.1515/9780773570917},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Barnard argues that Herder's concept of Humanitaet -- the fullest development of humanity's capacities in historically particular cultural forms -- constitutes a coherent alternative to Kantian universalism: one that grounds cross-cultural respect not in abstract rational norms but in the recognition of each culture's distinctive contribution to the range of human possibility.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most sustained philosophical account of Herder's alternative to Kant's cosmopolitan universalism. Essential for assessing whether Herder's particularism constitutes a genuine philosophical alternative or collapses into relativism -- a question directly relevant to evaluating Kant's 1784 essay and its critics.

  POSITION: Pro-Herder pluralism: defends Herder's qualified universalism as philosophically superior to both narrow nationalism and thin Enlightenment cosmopolitanism.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, Humanitaet, nationality, pluralism, universalism, High},
  abstract = {The core of J.G. Herder's philosophy of nationalism lies in the conviction that human creativity must be embedded in the particular culture of a communal language. While he acknowledged that this cultural particular must be integrated into a more universal humanity, he insisted that each culture should preserve its incommensurable distinctiveness. He also called for a new method of enquiry regarding history, one that demands empathetic sensitivity toward the uniquely individual while realizing that there are few gains without losses.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{sikka2007happiness,
  author = {Sikka, Sonia},
  title = {On the Value of Happiness: Herder contra Kant},
  journal = {Canadian Journal of Philosophy},
  year = {2007},
  volume = {37},
  number = {4},
  pages = {515--546},
  doi = {10.1353/cjp.2008.0005},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Sikka analyzes Kant's 1785 review of Herder's Ideen Part II, focusing on their disagreement about happiness as a goal of history. Herder argues that happiness is individual and culturally specific (a "child of practice, tradition, and custom") and that Kant's subordination of happiness to moral duty ignores real human ends; Kant responds that without a universal standard, Herder has no basis for claiming any historical progress at all.

  RELEVANCE: This article reconstructs the specific philosophical content of Kant's critical reviews of Herder with unusual precision. It shows that the happiness dispute is not merely about values but about the structure of historical teleology: whether there can be a single measure of historical progress or only culturally relative criteria.

  POSITION: Sympathetic to Herder: argues that Herder's individualism about happiness constitutes a genuine philosophical objection to Kant's subordination of well-being to a universal moral end.
  },
  keywords = {Kant-Herder-controversy, happiness, teleology, universalism, High},
  abstract = {In November of 1785, Kant published a highly unsympathetic review of the second part of Johann Gottfried Herder's Ideas for a Philosophy of the History of Mankind. Herder had once been Kant's pupil, and had greatly admired his teacher, but the content of this review shows how profound the philosophical differences between them had by then become. A central area of dispute emerging from the review concerns the nature of happiness, and its place within the 'destiny' or 'vocation' of the human race. Kant is responding, in particular, to a section of the Ideas entitled: 'The happiness of human beings is everywhere an individual good; consequently, it is everywhere climatic and organic, a child of practice, tradition, and custom.' Although Kant is not mentioned by name in this section, it clearly contains critical rejoinders, often quite harsh in tone, to aspects of his practical philosophy and philosophy of history, as Herder understands them.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{sikka2011herder,
  author = {Sikka, Sonia},
  title = {Herder on Humanity and Cultural Difference: Enlightened Relativism},
  year = {2011},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  address = {Cambridge},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9780511783012},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Sikka offers a comprehensive reading of Herder's social and political thought as a form of "enlightened relativism" -- a position that combines recognition of genuine cultural difference and incommensurability with a minimal universal commitment to Humanitaet as the common end of all human cultures. The book situates Herder's ethics, anthropology, philosophy of language, and philosophy of history within a unified interpretive framework.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the philosophical resources for assessing whether Herder's alternative to Kantian universalism in the 1784-91 Ideen is consistent and defensible. The "qualified relativism" thesis is directly relevant to evaluating whether Herder's pluralism is superior to, or merely the mirror image of, the problems in Kant's universalism.

  POSITION: Defends Herder as a philosophically coherent "qualified relativist" whose position occupies the space between cultural imperialism and self-undermining relativism.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, relativism, Humanitaet, cultural-difference, pluralism, High},
  abstract = {Herder is often criticized for having embraced cultural relativism, but there has been little philosophical discussion of what he actually wrote about the nature of the human species and its differentiation through culture. This book focuses on Herder's idea of culture, seeking to situate his social and political theses within the context of his anthropology, metaphysics, epistemology, ethics, theory of language and philosophy of history. It argues for a view of Herder as a qualified relativist, who combined the conception of a common human nature with a belief in the importance of culture in developing and shaping that nature. Especially highlighted are Herder's understanding of the relativity of virtue and happiness, and his belief in the impossibility of constructing a single best society.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{nassar2015analogy,
  author = {Nassar, Dalia},
  title = {Analogy, Natural History and the Philosophy of Nature: Kant, Herder and the Problem of Empirical Science},
  journal = {Journal of the Philosophy of History},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {9},
  number = {2},
  pages = {240--257},
  doi = {10.1163/18722636-12341302},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Nassar reconstructs Kant's 1785 methodological objections to Herder's use of analogy in the Ideen, arguing that Herder's reliance on analogical inference between humans and other natural beings drew deeply mistaken conclusions by overlooking fundamental differences. Crucially, Nassar shows that Kant's critique forced him into an "untenable situation" of granting analogy greater significance in his own subsequent work while also restricting its use -- a tension that shaped the Critique of Judgment.

  RELEVANCE: This paper establishes that the Kant-Herder dispute had direct consequences for Kant's development of critical teleology. The methodological controversy over analogy is not peripheral but central to understanding why Kant's philosophy of history and his account of natural teleology developed as they did, making the dispute constitutive for the late critical philosophy.

  POSITION: Balanced: does not adjudicate the methodological dispute but shows that Herder's challenge was philosophically productive for Kant's own development.
  },
  keywords = {Kant-Herder-controversy, analogy, teleology, natural-history, methodology, High},
  abstract = {In 1785 Kant published a series of critical reviews of Johann Gottfried Herder's Ideas for a Philosophy of the History of Humanity (1784-1785), in which he not only challenges Herder's conception of nature but also, and more importantly, his methodology. Kant's complaint is that by relying on analogy, Herder draws deeply mistaken conclusions that overlook fundamental differences between human and nonhuman beings. But was Kant's critique of Herder entirely fair? And how does it compare to Kant's own use of analogy? My claim is that Herder's use of analogy posed a fundamental methodological challenge to Kant, a challenge he sought to meet in the years following the reviews. In so doing, however, Kant found himself in the untenable situation of, on the one hand, granting analogy greater significance, and, on the other, severely restricting its use.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{beiser2011historicist,
  author = {Beiser, Frederick C.},
  title = {The German Historicist Tradition},
  year = {2011},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  address = {Oxford},
  doi = {10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199691555.001.0001},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Beiser traces the historicist tradition in German philosophy from its origins in Herder and the Enlightenment through Ranke, Droysen, and Dilthey, arguing that the central problem is whether history can be understood scientifically while respecting the individuality and meaning of historical cultures. The book provides a sustained account of Herder's philosophical innovations -- including his critique of Kant's abstract universalism -- as the founding moment of historicism.

  RELEVANCE: Places the Kant-Herder debate within the broader context of the emergence of historicism as a philosophical tradition. Beiser's framework helps clarify what is at stake in the dispute: whether a philosophy of history can combine objective scientific claims with genuine sensitivity to historical particularity.

  POSITION: Moderately pro-Herder on the historicist tradition, arguing that Herder's attention to individuality and cultural meaning makes him the true founder of historicism against Kant's more abstract universalism.
  },
  keywords = {historicism, hermeneutics, philosophy-of-history, Herder, German-idealism, Medium},
  abstract = {This is the first history in English of German historicism, the intellectual tradition which holds that history is the key to understanding all human values, beliefs and actions. Beiser surveys the key thinkers from the mid-18th to the early 20th century and illuminates the sources and reasons for this revolution in modern thought.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{mertel2016historicism,
  author = {Mertel, Kurt C. M.},
  title = {Historicism and Critique in {H}erder's {\em Another Philosophy of History}: Some Hermeneutic Reflections},
  journal = {European Journal of Philosophy},
  year = {2014},
  volume = {24},
  number = {2},
  pages = {397--416},
  doi = {10.1111/ejop.12088},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mertel examines Herder's 1774 essay "Also a Philosophy of History" (Auch eine Philosophie) -- the work that directly preceded and anticipated the Ideen -- arguing that Herder develops a form of historicist critique that is neither simple relativism nor naive particularism, but a hermeneutic stance that accepts the embeddedness of all historical understanding while retaining a critical edge against Enlightenment pretensions to universality.

  RELEVANCE: Important for understanding the philosophical continuity between Herder's early critique of Enlightenment in 1774 and the later Ideen that directly confronted Kant. Establishes that Herder's historical thinking has a critical, not merely descriptive, dimension -- which complicates simple readings of him as a relativist critic of progress.

  POSITION: Defends a hermeneutic reading of Herder that sees his historicism as philosophically coherent and not reducible to the relativism Kant attributed to him.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, historicism, hermeneutics, Also-a-Philosophy-of-History, Enlightenment, Medium},
  abstract = {In Another Philosophy of History, Herder claims that his aim is not to compare and judge different cultures, but merely to describe and explain how each came into being and thus to adopt the standpoint of an impartial observer. Despite Herder's stated aims, he is nevertheless unable to avoid justifying premodern forms of life and making context-transcending evaluative judgments in the process of trying to understand cultures on their own terms. This tension presents the challenge of accounting for it in the most charitable and illuminating way. The article argues this goal can be achieved by appealing to the resources of philosophical hermeneutics, which enables disclosure of two enabling conditions for Herder's project of which he was not explicitly aware: the internal connection between understanding and justification, and the enabling character of prejudice as the condition for the possibility of understanding as such.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{james2022enlightenment,
  author = {James, David},
  title = {Situating the {E}nlightenment in {H}erder's Philosophy of History},
  journal = {Journal of the Philosophy of History},
  year = {2022},
  volume = {16},
  number = {3},
  pages = {247--270},
  doi = {10.1163/18722636-12341480},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: James argues that, despite his overt criticism of Enlightenment abstraction, Herder's own philosophy of history commits him to treating the Enlightenment age as making a distinctive contribution to the development of humanity. Crucially, this contribution cannot make the Enlightenment superior to other ages; instead, abstract thinking and hostility to prejudice are among the forms that human powers take in Herder's universal survey. This internal tension reveals how Herder attempts to incorporate rather than simply reject Enlightenment norms.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the question of how consistent Herder's anti-Enlightenment stance is, showing that his philosophy of history is more complex than a simple anti-Kantian particularism. The internal tension James identifies is relevant to assessing whether Herder's alternative to Kant is genuinely coherent.

  POSITION: Interpretively neutral but philosophically critical: exposes an internal tension in Herder's philosophy of history without adjudicating whether Kant or Herder has the better position.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, Enlightenment, philosophy-of-history, particularism, universalism, Medium},
  abstract = {Although Herder is critical of the Enlightenment, I show that his philosophy of history commits him to the claim that the age and culture shaped by the Enlightenment in some way makes a distinctive contribution to the development of humanity. Yet this contribution cannot make this age and culture superior to earlier ones, for this would violate Herder's commitment to the principle that each age and culture ought to be accorded an equal status because of the equal value of its contribution to the full development of humanity. I argue that an abstract way of thinking and a hostility to prejudice are integral elements of Herder's attempt to situate the Enlightenment in his philosophy of history, despite how he otherwise criticizes the Enlightenment for its abstract thinking and its hostility to prejudice.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{mcnulty2022teleology,
  author = {McNulty, Michael Bennett},
  title = {A Science for Gods, a Science for Humans: Kant on Teleological Speculations in Natural History},
  journal = {Studies in History and Philosophy of Science},
  year = {2022},
  volume = {94},
  pages = {47--55},
  doi = {10.1016/j.shpsa.2022.04.008},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: McNulty argues that Kant himself engaged in teleological speculations in natural history -- particularly in his essays on race -- despite dismissing Herder's and Buffon's developmental histories as "daring adventures of reason." Kant's own practice requires hypothesizing about nature's purposes to explain the unity of species despite observable variation, threading a needle between purely mechanical accounts (which cannot ground species unity) and the "freewheeling" speculative narratives he attributes to Herder.

  RELEVANCE: Complicates the standard reading of Kant's 1785 reviews of Herder as simply rejecting teleological speculation in natural history. If Kant himself engages in analogous speculation, the methodological contrast between Kant and Herder is less sharp than the reviews suggest, and the dispute may partly reflect Kant's unwillingness to acknowledge his own reliance on similar moves.

  POSITION: Critically interrogates Kant's self-presentation in the Herder reviews by showing that his own natural history essays rely on similar teleological speculations.
  },
  keywords = {Kant, teleology, natural-history, race, Herder, methodology, Medium},
  abstract = {Kant's forthright rejection of rampant, ungrounded speculations in natural science, including in natural history, is well known. For instance, he dismisses the wild, unwarranted developmental histories of other early natural historians, such as Buffon and Herder, as "daring adventures of reason." However, as I show in this paper, Kant himself made use of teleological speculations in natural history, particularly in his three essays on race. I argue that, for Kant, speculations about nature's purposes are necessary to explain and to buttress the unification of organisms in real species, despite observable and heritable variations among members of these species. Without hypothesizing about nature's intentions, a mechanical, efficient-causal account of nature cannot appropriately ground the unity of species.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{helbig2016epigenesis,
  author = {Helbig, Daniela and Nassar, Dalia},
  title = {The Metaphor of Epigenesis: {K}ant, {B}lumenbach and {H}erder},
  journal = {Studies in History and Philosophy of Science Part A},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {58},
  pages = {98--107},
  doi = {10.1016/j.shpsa.2016.05.003},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Helbig and Nassar examine how epigenesis -- the theory of organic development from within -- functions as a key metaphor in Kant, Blumenbach, and Herder, with importantly different valences. For Herder, epigenetic organization is the model for cultural and historical development (each Volk growing organically from within); for Kant, epigenesis requires strict limits and is carefully distinguished from mere analogy; Blumenbach provides an intermediate case.

  RELEVANCE: Illuminates the organic naturalism at the center of Herder's philosophy of history and the precise point at which Kant objects to it: the extension of a biological model to historical and cultural development without critical restriction. Understanding the epigenesis dispute clarifies what Herder means by organic historical development in the Ideen.

  POSITION: Primarily reconstructive: maps the differences between the three thinkers' uses of epigenetic concepts, without adjudicating the larger dispute.
  },
  keywords = {epigenesis, organism, natural-history, Kant-Herder-controversy, Blumenbach, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@incollection{zuckert2011history,
  author = {Zuckert, Rachel},
  title = {History, Biology, and Philosophical Anthropology in {K}ant and {H}erder},
  booktitle = {Philosophie und Wissenschaft / Philosophy and Science},
  year = {2011},
  pages = {38--59},
  publisher = {De Gruyter},
  doi = {10.1515/9783110222869.38},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Zuckert examines how the distinction between history and biology structures the disagreement between Kant and Herder about philosophical anthropology. Herder integrates natural history and human history into a single organic framework; Kant insists on a sharp distinction between the causal-mechanical explanation appropriate to nature and the teleological-moral framing required for human history. The chapter argues that this distinction has fundamental consequences for what a "science of man" can legitimately claim.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a philosophically precise account of the structural difference between Kant's and Herder's approaches to human history, going beyond the textual controversy to the underlying metaphysical commitments. Useful for understanding why Kant thought Herder's naturalism was philosophically objectionable rather than merely methodologically lax.

  POSITION: Sympathetic to Kant's distinction between natural and human history, arguing that Herder's naturalism collapses a philosophically important boundary.
  },
  keywords = {philosophical-anthropology, history, biology, Kant-Herder-controversy, natural-history, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{denby2005herder,
  author = {Denby, David},
  title = {Herder: Culture, Anthropology and the {E}nlightenment},
  journal = {History of the Human Sciences},
  year = {2005},
  volume = {18},
  number = {1},
  pages = {55--76},
  doi = {10.1177/0952695105051126},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Denby argues that Herder's anthropology is more closely related to Enlightenment thought than usually acknowledged: his critique of Enlightenment abstraction is unstable because his own concept of Kultur as human development retains a unitary model of civilization. Herder's relativism is open to question since he in fact applies transcultural criteria for judgment, making his position closer to a qualified Enlightenment universalism than the simple pluralism it is often taken to be.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a revisionist reading that complicates the standard contrast between Herder (pluralist, anti-Enlightenment) and Kant (universalist, Enlightenment). If Herder's position is internally unstable, the Kant-Herder dispute may not be the clean contrast between universalism and particularism it is often portrayed as.

  POSITION: Critical of both standard readings of Herder as simple relativist and simple anti-Enlightenment thinker; argues for a more complex relationship between Herder and Enlightenment thought.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, Enlightenment, anthropology, relativism, culture, Medium},
  abstract = {The anthropological sensibility has often been seen as growing out of opposition to Enlightenment universalism. Johann Gottfried Herder (1744-1803) is often cited as an ancestor of modern cultural relativism, in which cultures exist in the plural. This article argues that Herder's anthropology, and anthropology generally, are more closely related to Enlightenment thought than is generally considered. Herder certainly attacks Enlightenment abstraction, the arrogance of its Eurocentric historical teleology, and argues the case for a proto-hermeneutical approach which emphasizes embeddedness, horizon, the usefulness of prejudice. But a comparison with a central Enlightenment figure like the natural historian Buffon (1708-88) reveals shared anthropological assumptions. Herder's critique of progress is thus an unstable one: culture as the process through which humanity develops can also be called Enlightenment. Herder's definition of culture is much closer to a unitary Enlightenment model of civilization than is frequently suggested.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{gjesdal2017hermeneutics,
  author = {Gjesdal, Kristin},
  title = {Herder's Hermeneutics: History, Poetry, Enlightenment},
  year = {2017},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  address = {Cambridge},
  doi = {10.1017/9781316285770},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Gjesdal offers a systematic reconstruction of Herder's hermeneutics -- his theory of historical understanding and interpretation -- arguing that it represents a coherent philosophical alternative to both Enlightenment rationalism and later Romantic irrationalism. The book traces how Herder's engagement with poetry, history, and critique of Kantian philosophy generates a distinctive approach to understanding historically situated cultural forms.

  RELEVANCE: Herder's hermeneutics is the methodological underpinning of his philosophy of history: his insistence on empathetic understanding and historical situatedness is the methodological counterpart to his substantive claim that each culture has its own Humanitaet. Gjesdal's account helps clarify what philosophical resources Herder brings to his dispute with Kant.

  POSITION: Sympathetic reconstruction: argues that Herder's hermeneutics represents a philosophically coherent and productive alternative to Kantian critical philosophy.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, hermeneutics, history, Enlightenment, understanding, Low},
  abstract = {Through a detailed study of Herder's Enlightenment thought, especially his philosophy of literature, Kristin Gjesdal offers a reading of the historical origins and contemporary challenges of modern hermeneutics. She shows that hermeneutic philosophy grew out of a historical, anthropological, and poetic discourse in the mid-eighteenth century and argues that, as such, it represents a rich engagement with the potentials and limits of human meaning and understanding. Gjesdal's study broadens our conception of hermeneutic philosophy and underlines the importance of Herder's contribution to the development of this discipline.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{schmidt2025legacy,
  author = {Schmidt, Johannes},
  title = {The Lost Legacy of {J}ohann {G}ottfried {H}erder},
  journal = {The European Legacy},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {30},
  number = {6},
  pages = {653--676},
  doi = {10.1080/10848770.2025.2525604},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Schmidt argues that Herder's philosophical legacy has been systematically undervalued in both philosophical and intellectual history, tracing the mechanisms by which Herder was marginalized after his death -- including Kant's and Goethe's hostility, the triumph of German Idealism, and subsequent historiographic choices. The article argues for recovering Herder's contributions to philosophy of history, language, and anthropology as genuinely original and not reducible to proto-nationalism.

  RELEVANCE: Offers a recent (2025) reassessment of the long-term reception of the Kant-Herder dispute, useful for understanding how the terms of the debate have been shaped by reception history rather than purely by the philosophical merits of each position.

  POSITION: Advocates for renewed attention to Herder's philosophical contributions, arguing that the historiographic marginalization of Herder has distorted the philosophical record.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, reception-history, lost-legacy, philosophy-of-history, Low, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}
